From 5b1ba42366ca5a86053bfb97beb88c0565af3c14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 07:23:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ship uBlock Origin 1.75.0 and move profiles over to it

The Chrome Web Store stopped carrying Manifest V2 extensions on 31 August
2026, so the store copy Bare shipped, cjpalhdlnbpafiamejdnhcphjbkeiagm, can no
longer be updated from anywhere. The author publishes his own signed builds on
GitHub, and those carry his key, so they have another id:
fkgkibajhfbepljeaefdnfnegdcjomkh. The loader now ships 1.75.0 under that id.

Swapping one id for the other naively would be destructive. The provider
would stop claiming the store copy and uninstall it with everything the user
had set, and install a fresh copy beside nothing. So a profile that has the
store copy installed by Bare is carried over first:

- uBlock Origin keeps all of its state in chrome.storage.local: filter lists,
  the user's own filters, trusted sites, dynamic rules, and the version it last
  ran as. That LevelDB directory is copied to the new id's place on the storage
  backend's own sequence, so no write from the running extension lands halfway
  through. With the version carried along, the new copy starts as an upgrade
  rather than a first run.
- What Chromium keeps outside the extension is recorded and applied to the new
  copy once it has loaded: switched off by the user, incognito access, file
  access, its place among the pinned extensions, and site access.
- Only once the copy has worked is the store copy left unclaimed, so the
  provider retires it. If anything fails, the store copy stays claimed and
  working, and the next launch tries again.

The cases that must not bring the blocker back are kept apart: a profile that
declined it on the welcome screen, a user who removed the store copy, and a
user who installed the store copy themselves, who would otherwise end up with
two blockers running.

The new copy's update_url is the author's update file on jsDelivr rather than
clients2.google.com, so the periodic extension update check for it no longer
goes to Google.
